-- postpartum week 45: Feeding yourself well without spending more
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   45,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Feeding yourself well without spending more',
   '"Good nourishment does not have to depend on expensive foods — many of the most dependable foods are already everyday staples."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about the everyday, often more affordable foods that already do a lot for you — and letting go of the idea that healthy has to mean expensive. A year of new expenses — for the baby, medical visits, everything that comes with this stage — often puts real pressure on the food budget too. Dal, eggs, curd, seasonal produce and locally available staples are often among the more economical sources of protein and nourishment, although prices and availability vary by location, season and household. None of them are a downgrade from something fancier — they''re simply doing the job well, often at a lower cost.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: lower-cost foods such as dal, seasonal vegetables, eggs and staple grains can fit into many diabetes meal plans, but portions, carbohydrate amounts and meal timing still need to follow your individual plan. Cutting back on meals because of cost can affect glucose management; tell your treating team if budget pressure is making regular meals difficult. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: check that a cheaper substitute is actually safe for you before switching — a lower-cost alternative isn''t automatically allergen-free, and packaged budget brands can vary in their ingredients. If you''re regularly unable to afford enough food for yourself, that''s a real and common concern worth raising — with a partner, a trusted family member, or your care team — rather than carrying alone. It isn''t a personal failing.',
   'Depending on local prices and access, eggs, dal, curd and other familiar staples may provide economical protein options. Eggs and chicken can be included where they fit your household''s budget, while fish choices should depend on local cost, availability and lower-mercury guidance. Keep fish to two or three servings a week, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A year of new expenses — for the baby, medical visits, everything that comes with this stage — often puts real pressure on the food budget too. This week isn''t about cutting corners on your own nutrition. It''s about noticing which foods already do double duty: genuinely affordable and genuinely worth eating.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with peanuts", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable sandwich with a glass of milk", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg bhurji with rice and a cooked seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with roasted chana or peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk with a small handful of roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The everyday staples that already do the most", "notes": "Dal, eggs, curd, seasonal produce and locally available staples are often among the more economical sources of protein and nourishment, although prices and availability vary. None of them need to be treated as a downgrade from something fancier; they''re simply doing the job well, often at a lower cost.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a small seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi with curd", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk and vegetable curry with rice", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Frozen and dried aren''t a downgrade", "notes": "Plain frozen vegetables can be a useful alternative when fresh produce is expensive, unavailable or likely to spoil before you use it. They remain nutritious choices; compare local prices and, where possible, choose versions without added sauces or seasoning. Dried beans and lentils, bought in bulk and soaked at home, are typically cheaper than pre-cooked or packaged versions of the same food, and tinned fish can be a shelf-stable protein option where a locally affordable, lower-mercury variety is available.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-stuffed paratha with curd", "lunch_a": "Rice with rajma curry made from dried, soaked rajma and a cooked seasonal vegetable", "lunch_b": "A locally affordable lower-mercury fish or egg curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of cooked sprouted moong chaat", "dinner_a": "Roti with a frozen mixed-vegetable curry and dal", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew with rice and a frozen-vegetable mix", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What ''superfood'' pricing doesn''t tell you", "notes": "A food isn''t more nutritious just because it''s imported, trending, or expensive. Many familiar, everyday foods already grown and eaten locally — dal, seasonal greens, curd, eggs — provide the same kinds of nutrients that a costlier ''superfood'' is often marketed for.", "breakfast_a": "Rava upma with vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Masala egg curry with paratha", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a lauki-chana dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-dal curry with rice and a seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with a spoon of roasted peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of milk with roasted chana", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi with ghee", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk and vegetable curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few dates"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Reducing waste without extra effort", "notes": "Reducing waste can mean using clean, edible parts of vegetables that might otherwise be overlooked, freezing a safe portion before it spoils, and buying only what you realistically expect to cook. Wash produce thoroughly under running water, remove damaged areas, and discard any peel, stem, leaf or other part that is not normally considered edible.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable roll", "lunch_a": "Rice with dal containing finely chopped, thoroughly washed edible vegetable leaves or stems, with a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with buttermilk (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "A dal-and-vegetable soup made with clean edible vegetable trimmings, with toast", "dinner_b": "A locally affordable lower-mercury fish curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When the budget genuinely feels tight", "notes": "Some weeks and months, the honest answer is that money is tight, not just habits worth adjusting. That''s a real pressure, not a personal failing. If you haven''t already, it''s worth checking what government nutrition and maternal-health schemes you may be eligible for — several are covered in this app''s Wealth section — and worth talking to your care team if you''re genuinely going without.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk and chopped banana, with sweetness adjusted to preference", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg with roti and a seasonal vegetable or fruit", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with roti and a seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Guava with roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with roasted peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Mixed dal khichdi with a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Rajma curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Which food on your plate this week gave you the most for the least — money, effort, or both?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What is one affordable staple you would like to keep stocked more consistently?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 46: When a fast is part of your faith
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   46,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'When a fast is part of your faith',
   '"Your faith and your baby''s needs don''t have to be in conflict — informed choice and a little preparation can help you protect both."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about religious and cultural fasting — a real part of many mothers'' lives — and practical, appropriately cautious information that supports your own informed choice, not a directive either way. Many mothers observe a religious or cultural fast at some point during this stage — Karva Chauth, Navratri, Ekadashi, Ramzan roza, Lent, or another tradition specific to your own faith and family. This week isn''t about telling you whether to fast. It offers practical, appropriately cautious information to support your decision. If you have a medical condition or health concern, discuss the proposed fast with your care team; if you want guidance about religious rules or alternatives, consult a trusted authority within your own tradition. The daily menus below remain complete nourishment options for you. On a non-fasting day, use them in the usual pattern. If your tradition restricts when or what you eat, use only the options that fit your practice, and combine them within the permitted eating window rather than treating every listed meal as something to fit into a fasting day.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: fasting can cause low or high glucose, dehydration and, for some people, serious complications. Do not independently skip, delay or change medication to fit a fast. Before fasting, obtain an individual plan covering medication timing, glucose monitoring and the level at which you must stop the fast. If glucose becomes too low or too high, or you develop symptoms of a hypo, marked hyperglycaemia or illness, break the fast and follow your established treatment plan. If you currently have anaemia, remain symptomatic, are taking treatment for iron deficiency or had significant blood loss and have not been told that you have recovered, a prolonged fast may make it harder to meet your food and fluid needs. Discuss whether the proposed fast is suitable for your present health.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. Keep fish to two or three servings a week, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Begin by identifying the kind of fast you are considering: whether it restricts timing, fluids, particular foods, or some combination of these. The practical plan depends on what the fast actually involves.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg with toast and a seasonal vegetable or fruit", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with a glass of milk", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with buttermilk (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a mixed-vegetable curry and dal", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What research says about fasting and your milk", "notes": "Research on fasting during breastfeeding is limited and mostly relates to Ramadan. Small studies have not consistently found major short-term changes in infant growth or the main components of breast milk, but the evidence is not strong enough to promise that fasting will affect every mother and baby in the same way. Individual responses can differ, particularly with long fasting hours, hot weather, illness, low intake or difficulty replacing fluids and food during the eating window.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable paratha", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with dates", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal khichdi with a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Not all fasts are the same", "notes": "Fasting traditions vary widely, and knowing which kind you''re considering matters. Some are complete, waterless fasts for a set period. Others allow water, milk, fruit or specific foods throughout the day. Others are time-restricted — no food or water during daylight hours, but a full meal before dawn and after sunset. Each can carry a different level of risk for a breastfeeding mother. Religious rules, exemptions and modifications differ between traditions, communities and individual circumstances. If you want religious guidance, consult a trusted authority within your own tradition.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji with roti", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken stew with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped seasonal fruit with curd, or with roasted chana as an adult portion—keep whole roasted chana and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao with raita", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Fasting while breastfeeding an older baby", "notes": "At this stage, your baby may be eating complementary foods while continuing to breastfeed. That can change the practical picture, but it does not make every fast automatically suitable. Your own health, milk supply, the type and length of the fast, the weather, your baby''s usual feeding pattern and any medical condition still matter. Continue following your baby''s established feeding and breastfeeding cues rather than changing the baby''s intake simply to make a fast possible.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Fruit with curd and seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Boiled potato with curd and roasted peanuts, if permitted within your practice (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Sabudana khichdi with curd", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you choose to fast", "notes": "If you do choose to fast, a few practical habits can help: choose nourishing food and fluids in whatever window your tradition allows, avoid pushing yourself through heavy chores or exertion that day, and pace yourself rather than eating a very large meal all at once. Continue responding to your baby''s usual breastfeeding and complementary-feeding cues. Do not force additional feeds or change the baby''s established feeding plan solely to compensate for the fast. If your practice restricts which foods you eat rather than when you eat, build a complete plate from what''s allowed within your own tradition — for example combining a grain like sabudana or singhara atta with a protein source like curd, milk or nuts, and fruit — rather than relying mainly on sabudana, potato, fruit or sweet foods alone. If your tradition allows eating before and after a time-restricted fast, a simple pattern can help: - Before the fast: choose a meal containing a staple grain or other carbohydrate, a protein food, vegetables or fruit, and fluids. - After the fast: begin with fluids and a comfortable amount of food, then eat a balanced meal containing protein, carbohydrate and vegetables rather than relying mainly on fried or sugary foods. - If another eating opportunity is available: add curd, milk, fruit, dal, egg, roasted chana or another suitable protein-containing option.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with milk and chopped fruit", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with roasted chana or curd (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Banana with milk or curd", "dinner_a": "Roti with a lauki-chana dal and curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few dates"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Signs to break the fast, or check in with your care team", "notes": "Stop the fast, drink, eat as needed and seek prompt medical advice if you faint, become confused, cannot keep fluids down, pass very little urine, develop concerning glucose readings, or feel significantly unwell. Seek advice if your baby feeds markedly less than usual, has fewer wet diapers than usual, seems unusually sleepy or unwell, or if you have an ongoing concern about milk intake or growth. Choosing to stop or adapt a fast for your health, or your baby''s, is a reasonable and common decision.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with peanuts and curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion uttapam", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a seasonal vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk with roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Seasonal citrus fruit with curd or paneer", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi with ghee", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Whatever your own relationship with fasting looks like — whether you fast, modify it, or don''t — how did this week''s information change how you think about it, if at all?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'If you ever choose to fast, what support, preparation or modification would help you protect your health without feeling that you had failed your faith?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 47: Eating well when you're the one who's sick
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   47,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well when you''re the one who''s sick',
   '"You don''t have to eat perfectly while you''re unwell — just enough, often enough, to keep going."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. This week offers low-effort nourishment ideas for times when appetite and energy are reduced. It does not diagnose an illness or replace treatment for persistent fever, breathing difficulty, significant dehydration, mastitis or another condition requiring medical care. Being sick as a mother is different — there''s rarely a real day off, you may still be breastfeeding, and your appetite might be the last thing that feels manageable. This week is about practical, low-effort nourishment when you''re unwell, not a full recovery protocol. Little and often beats a perfect plate.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: illness — especially with vomiting, reduced eating, or fever — can genuinely affect your glucose in either direction. Don''t stop or change your medication on your own; ask your care team for a sick-day plan in advance, including how often to check your glucose and when to seek help. If you have kidney, heart or another condition for which you have been given a fluid limit: follow that individual plan rather than general advice to drink more. If you suspect mastitis and are becoming significantly unwell, notice redness spreading rapidly, suspect an abscess, or see no improvement after about 12 to 24 hours of supportive care, contact your care team promptly.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety, and are easy to soften into softer, easier-to-manage forms when you''re not feeling well. Keep fish to two or three servings a week, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today, identify two meals that require almost no decision-making and one person you could ask for practical help. A written backup plan is easier to use when illness has already reduced your energy.", "breakfast_a": "Suji chilla with curd", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg with toast and a seasonal fruit or cooked vegetable", "lunch_a": "Roti with a toor dal and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with curd or roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole roasted chana and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk with roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole roasted chana and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi with ghee", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "You can (usually) keep breastfeeding", "notes": "With many common illnesses — including a cold, influenza, COVID-19, diarrhoea and mastitis — breastfeeding can usually continue. However, it is too broad to say that infections never pass through breast milk. Different infections require different precautions. Respiratory illnesses such as influenza spread mainly through close contact and respiratory droplets rather than through breast milk. Wash your hands before touching your baby or expressing milk, cover coughs and sneezes, clean pumping equipment carefully, and consider a mask when you have respiratory symptoms and close contact cannot be avoided. Tell the pharmacist or prescriber that you are breastfeeding before using a new medicine, including an over-the-counter cold remedy.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with toast", "lunch_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken pulao with a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with curd or roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole roasted chana and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Warm milk with toast or a banana", "dinner_a": "Dal-and-vegetable soup with toast", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Mastitis is different from a cold", "notes": "Mastitis is inflammation of the breast and may cause a painful, swollen, red or warm area, sometimes with fever or flu-like symptoms. Continue breastfeeding responsively if you can, including from the affected breast, but do not try to force the breast empty or express more milk than your baby needs. If feeding is too painful, hand-express only enough for comfort or to provide the milk your baby would ordinarily take. Rest, use a cold compress for comfort, avoid deep massage or firm pressure, and seek advice about pain relief that is appropriate for you. Seek medical care sooner if you are becoming significantly unwell, redness is spreading rapidly, you suspect an abscess, or symptoms are worsening. If there is no improvement after approximately 12 to 24 hours of supportive care, contact a clinician.", "breakfast_a": "Dalia porridge with milk", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette with roti", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Curd with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Banana with curd", "dinner_a": "Curd rice with a small portion of soft-cooked dal or a cooked vegetable, as tolerated", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole nuts, peanuts and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Eating when you have no appetite", "notes": "Older advice about starving a fever or sticking to strict, bland foods has largely been replaced by simpler guidance: eat what you can tolerate, in small amounts, as often as you can manage. A few days of simple, repetitive meals — khichdi, toast, curd rice — is completely fine. This isn''t the week to worry about variety. A day or two of simpler food can be reasonable. As appetite returns, move back toward your usual balanced meals rather than remaining on a highly restricted \"bland\" diet without a medical reason.", "breakfast_a": "Soft moong-dal khichdi", "breakfast_b": "Toast with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Curd rice", "lunch_b": "Shredded chicken with soft rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "Plain toast", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal khichdi with ghee", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable soup with toast", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Replacing fluids when illness reduces your intake", "notes": "When fever, vomiting or diarrhoea causes fluid loss, take frequent small sips of fluids you can tolerate. If vomiting or diarrhoea is significant, an oral rehydration solution can help replace both fluid and electrolytes lost through significant vomiting or diarrhoea. Use a packaged ORS product prepared with exactly the amount of safe water stated on its label. Seek medical help if you cannot retain fluids or develop signs of significant dehydration.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal and rice congee", "breakfast_b": "Dal-and-vegetable soup with toast", "lunch_a": "Soft rice with watery moong dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-rice soup", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk or curd, if tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "Packaged ORS prepared exactly as directed, when vomiting or diarrhoea is causing fluid loss", "dinner_a": "Dal-and-vegetable soup with toast", "dinner_b": "Egg-drop and vegetable soup with toast", "still_hungry": "A banana, curd or another simple food you tolerate"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to get medical help", "notes": "Seek urgent medical care for breathing difficulty, chest pain, confusion, fainting, blue or grey lips, severe dehydration, blood in vomit or stool, or if you appear seriously unwell. Seek prompt advice if vomiting prevents you from keeping fluids down, fever is persistent or worsening, or symptoms are not improving as expected. For your baby at this stage, seek urgent medical advice for breathing difficulty, unusual difficulty waking, signs of significant dehydration, markedly reduced feeding, persistent vomiting, or whenever the baby appears seriously unwell. Follow local paediatric guidance for fever and illness. It''s always reasonable to call for guidance rather than wait and wonder.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg with roti", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Nimbu paani with roasted chana, if tolerated (adult portion — keep whole roasted chana and other hard foods out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Guava or another seasonal fruit with curd", "dinner_a": "Rice with a lauki-chana dal and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. However this week finds you — well or recovering — what''s one simple meal you could lean on the next time you''re not feeling your best?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Who could help protect one period of rest, prepare food, or take over part of the baby''s care when you are unwell?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
