-- postpartum week 51: Eating well as your baby's first birthday approaches
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   51,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well as your baby''s first birthday approaches',
   '"You''ve spent a year feeding everyone else — today''s a good day to remember your own plate too."',
   'Many families begin preparing for a baby''s first birthday around now — or may celebrate a little early — with sweets, guests, photographs and sometimes a ritual or puja. It is easy for the mother in the middle of everything to barely eat a proper meal herself. This week isn''t about restricting the celebration food. It''s about making sure a plate of your own is part of the day too, not an afterthought squeezed in once everyone else has been served. If you have diabetes, PCOS, or are otherwise watching your blood sugar, a day with concentrated sweets is worth a small amount of planning, not avoiding the celebration altogether. Whatever this milestone looks like for your family — a big celebration, a quiet day, or something in between — this week is simply about making sure you''re fed too. This remains a nourishment plan for the mother, not a birthday menu for the baby. If your baby is still under 12 months, do not offer honey or foods containing honey. Adult cake, mithai and party food are not automatically suitable for a baby. Keep whole nuts, seeds and hard, sticky or chewy sweets out of reach, and serve the baby''s food in a developmentally appropriate texture.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: follow your usual meal, monitoring and medication plan on a celebration day. Eating a sweet with or after a regular meal may be easier to manage than replacing a meal with sweets, but protein or fibre does not cancel the carbohydrate. Do not skip meals, medication or planned doses to compensate. If you have PCOS or prediabetes but do not use glucose-lowering medicine: you do not need to avoid the celebration. Continue regular meals and follow any individual glucose guidance you have already been given. If you''re breastfeeding: a busy hosting day can mean skipped meals and low fluid intake without you quite noticing, and that tends to affect your energy and comfort faster than on an ordinary day — keeping water within reach and eating at roughly your usual times helps. However your family marks this milestone — or doesn''t — that''s entirely yours to decide; this week is about your own plate, not a particular kind of celebration.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. Keep fish to two to three servings a week, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A year of feeding a baby often means a year of half-eaten meals, cold tea, and \"I''ll eat after.\" Today, notice whether that''s still true for you — and if it is, let today be the day you eat before you''re starving, not after.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal-and-vegetable adai with chutney", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor-vegetable curry and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken chettinad with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted pistachios (adult portion — keep whole pistachios out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a lauki-moong dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If you''re the one hosting", "notes": "If you''re the one organizing today — the guests, the sweets, the photos — it''s easy to end up eating standing up, in between everything else, or not at all until it''s over. Setting aside your own plate before the day gets busy is worth the two minutes it takes.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs with toast or roti and a fruit bowl", "lunch_a": "Rice with a mixed-vegetable sambar and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted makhana with a glass of milk (adult portion — keep whole makhana out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Seasonal fruit with roasted sunflower seeds (adult portion — keep whole seeds out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana curry and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Sweets don''t need to be earned or feared", "notes": "There may be cake, mithai or both, and that is completely fine. Skipping meals to \"make room\" can leave you excessively hungry and uncomfortable later. Let the sweet sit alongside or after a regular meal rather than replacing the meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with peanuts", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with paratha", "lunch_a": "Rajma-vegetable pulao with raita", "lunch_b": "Chicken biryani with raita", "nourishment_break_a": "A serving of mithai or cake that feels satisfying, enjoyed with or after a regular meal; roasted nuts may be added if wanted (adult portion for whole nuts — keep them out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Cucumber-and-mint raita", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken korma with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re managing your blood sugar today", "notes": "A celebration does not require you to avoid every sweet. Keep your usual meals as steady as the day allows and fit celebration food around the diabetes or glucose plan that normally works for you. Pairing carbohydrate with protein or fibre may help some people, but it does not make the carbohydrate disappear. Worth knowing: If you use insulin or another medicine that can cause low glucose, follow your established monitoring and medication plan. Do not skip a planned meal or dose to compensate for sweets. If celebration timing will substantially disrupt your usual routine, ask your diabetes team beforehand how to handle it.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla with mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana-lauki curry and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken stew with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana with a small apple (adult portion — keep whole chana out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer cubes with cucumber sticks (adult portion — keep firm cubes and sticks out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a masoor dal and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Rajma curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If today feels bigger than just a birthday", "notes": "A first birthday can bring up more than cake and photographs — pride, exhaustion, maybe a few unexpected tears, or a strange mix of all three as you look back on the year. However today feels, there''s no right way to feel it. Eating something yourself, even a small plate, helps you actually be present for whichever feelings show up.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong-masoor dal and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-potato curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole chana out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk with roasted pumpkin seeds (adult portion — keep whole seeds out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a green-moong dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Palak paneer with rice", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Tomorrow, it''s back to ordinary", "notes": "One day of celebration doesn''t undo a year of steady eating, and it doesn''t need a strict day-after either. If yesterday included more sugar or richer food than usual, the plan for today is simply an ordinary day — regular meals, plenty of water, nothing to prove.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with sambar or curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A boiled egg with a seasonal fruit", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with roasted sesame seeds (adult portion — keep whole seeds out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with an urad dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Matar paneer with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back on this past year of feeding a baby — and yourself, some days more than others — what''s one thing you''d want to carry forward, and one thing you''d want to let go of?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let today''s celebration include you, fully — not just as the one who fed everyone else?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 52: Completing your first year—and continuing to nourish yourself
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   52,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Completing your first year—and continuing to nourish yourself',
   '"A year of feeding a baby has also been a year of feeding yourself — that doesn''t end today."',
   'Fifty-two weeks ago, this series started with a simple idea: that the mother feeding a new baby needed feeding too. That idea doesn''t expire today. Whatever your situation is right now — still breastfeeding, weaned months ago, feeding formula, somewhere in between — is exactly right for you. There''s no single point everyone is supposed to have reached by a baby''s first birthday. If you''re still breastfeeding, that is entirely normal. WHO recommends continued breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for up to two years or beyond. Continuing is valid, reducing feeds is valid, and stopping is valid; this week does not rank those paths. If you''ve stopped breastfeeding, some nutrition needs may change, but there is no single across-the-board reset. Your appetite, menstrual cycle, diet, previous deficiencies, medical conditions and future-pregnancy plans all matter. Regular, varied meals remain useful regardless of the feeding stage. Recovery does not run on a strict calendar. Sleep, energy and pelvic or core symptoms can continue changing after the first year. But persistent or worsening fatigue, pain, heavy bleeding, low mood or marked hair shedding deserves attention rather than being dismissed as "just postpartum."',
   'If you''re breastfeeding beyond 12 months: WHO supports continued breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for up to two years or beyond. Continue regular, satisfying meals and drink according to thirst. The amount you need may still vary with feeding frequency, appetite, activity and individual health. If you''ve stopped breastfeeding or never breastfed: do not automatically stop, continue or reduce supplements simply because the calendar has reached one year. Iron needs can change when periods return, while calcium recommendations do not necessarily change. Review supplements according to your diet, symptoms, test results and care-team guidance. If you have anaemia, heavy periods or persistent fatigue: ask whether your blood count and iron status should be checked rather than assuming the first year has corrected everything. If you have thyroid disease, diabetes, prediabetes or PCOS: continue the monitoring and medication plan already made with your care team. Reaching one year postpartum is not a reason to change medication, supplements or carbohydrate targets independently. However you''re feeding your baby, and however you''re feeding yourself, right now — that''s the right starting point for whatever comes next, not a report card on the year behind you.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. Keep fish to two to three servings a week, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fifty-two weeks of this series come down to one plain idea: however you''re eating today is a fine place to be. Nothing about reaching this week requires you to have arrived anywhere in particular.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable sooji (rava) upma with roasted peanuts", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom omelette with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted cashews (adult portion — keep whole cashews out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk with a spoon of ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong-chana dal and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish moilee with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If you''re still breastfeeding", "notes": "Many mothers continue breastfeeding beyond the first birthday alongside complementary foods. If that remains comfortable for you and your child, your meals can continue in the same flexible pattern: regular, satisfying food, enough fluid and adjustments based on your actual hunger and feeding routine.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with sambar or curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum omelette with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a urad-moong dal and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton stew with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with roasted makhana (adult portion — keep whole makhana out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates with a glass of milk", "dinner_a": "Roti with a black urad dal and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you''ve stopped breastfeeding", "notes": "If breastfeeding has ended — recently or months ago — there is no automatic need to reduce portions or begin dieting. Appetite may change gradually, or not noticeably at first. Continue regular meals and adjust according to hunger, energy, activity, menstrual patterns and individual health.", "breakfast_a": "Methi-and-besan thepla with curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-egg bhurji with toast", "lunch_a": "Roti with a mixed-vegetable curry and dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable curry with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with an apple (adult portion — keep whole peanuts out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Guava with roasted sunflower seeds (adult portion — keep whole seeds out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a green-gram dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken tikka with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Recovery beyond the first year", "notes": "Reaching one year does not create a deadline for strength, sleep, mood or pelvic recovery. Some changes continue gradually. What matters is distinguishing ordinary variation from symptoms that remain persistent, worsen or interfere with daily life. Worth knowing: Seek healthcare support for persistent pain, heavy or unusual bleeding, pelvic pressure, leaking, low mood, breathlessness, marked fatigue or anything else that worries you. Most postpartum hair shedding improves by the first birthday; if substantial shedding continues or normal fullness has not returned, ask whether another cause should be assessed.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli with sambar or curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Egg-and-potato curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted pumpkin seeds (adult portion — keep whole seeds out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with a glass of buttermilk (adult portion — keep whole chana out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a tinda-moong dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum bhurji with roti", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The habits that outlast this year", "notes": "Eating enough, eating a variety, drinking water, and not treating food as something to earn or apologize for — none of that was ever really about \"postpartum.\" Those are just how eating well works, and they''re worth keeping long after any label attached to this year falls away.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with multigrain toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana curry and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with a banana (adult portion — keep whole peanuts out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a whole-moong dal and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry with roti and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Wherever you go from here", "notes": "This first year does not have to end with a graduation test. Keep whichever parts of these plans genuinely helped — regular meals, flexible alternatives, nourishment breaks or simply remembering to feed yourself. As Nourish moves into its next stage, the chart remains a guide, not a report card.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal khichdi with curd", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg with vegetable poha", "lunch_a": "Rice with a mixed-dal curry and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka with rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted makhana (adult portion — keep whole makhana out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with roasted chana (adult portion — keep whole chana out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana-spinach dal and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back across this first year, what''s one way of eating you would like to carry into the next stage — and one expectation you are ready to leave behind?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to keep feeding yourself well simply because you deserve it — not because you need to complete a stage perfectly?',
   'This week completes Nourish''s first postpartum year, not the whole journey. Next week, Nourish continues into the next stage with the same central promise: the plan can evolve as your life does, and you still deserve to be fed well.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
